{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 2, :comments ["<b>You shall not add to the matter.</b> For through your adding to my words with which I warned you — 'See, HaShem has set before you the land; go up, inherit it' — you added and said, 'Let us send men before us.' The addition caused your fathers' corpses to die in the wilderness." "<b>And you shall not diminish from it.</b> I said to you, 'Do not go up,' but you diminished my commandment — 'and the Amalekite and the Canaanite came out and struck them and crushed them.' Henceforth, take my commandments without addition or diminution.\\n\\nAnother interpretation: <b>You shall not add to the matter</b> etc. — an answer to sectarians among Israel who denigrated the Talmud and said: how did the sages of Israel add many things in the Talmud that are not in the Torah? Behold, it is written: <b>You shall not add to the matter, and you shall not diminish from it</b> — let them erase the additions from the book of life! Here is the answer to their words: this language appears in only two places in the Torah, and only regarding divinity and fear. That is to say, you must not add to the fear of HaShem another fear, nor diminish from His fear. Here in the portion Va'etchanan it is written <b>You shall not add to the matter</b>, and immediately afterward: 'Your eyes have seen what HaShem did at Baal Peor,' etc. And in the portion Re'eh it is written 'You shall not add to it and you shall not diminish from it,' and written just above, nearby: 'For even their sons and their daughters they burn in fire to their Elohim.' But regarding ordinary commandments, HaShem did not warn against adding, in order to make a fence and a safeguard for the Torah."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :chizkuni, :lang :en, :chapter 4, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:51:38.556734568Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}